s  far as just your your aura or the the  perceived way of how you feel during the  game now john gruden always made me  aware of that hey man you got to bring  some juice guys are looking to you you  know you got to stay positive you know  you know don't don't don't get down and  sulk when things are bad and added to  that too not only was i aware you know  of course i grew up in it but you know  when i went when the game was over i  wanted to go home and i wanted to sit on  the couch and watch the highlights and  of course i wanted to watch the  highlights even more if i played a  really good game but i was going to  watch them if i played bad too because i  wanted to look at what i looked like  what my body language and facial  expressions were and if they were  acceptable to me even though i don't  always like you know you i didn't like  to watch myself that way but  i knew those were important things and i  did want to have an awareness about  how  my actions and things like that  where they might be as far as affecting  the rest of the team  recent case in point also bears  quarterback jay cutler when adam gase  was there as the offensive coordinator  in 2015 one of the big challenges was to  get cutler to be sensitive to how he  came across right he'd  pull the chin strap off and have his  head down walking off the field and do  those things that create the perception  and then the reality that there's an  attitude problem there's a demeanor  problem and it does rub off on teammates  and and chris i i i feel weird i've been  praising aaron rodgers so much this year  but he deserves it that monday night  game against the lions yeah all those  shots of his face after guys were  dropping passes right and he managed to  keep that serenity now expression right  and it it's important it's critical and  it worked because the the look he knows  how to work with his players and his  teammates and if you start making faces  and grimacing they're going to know  about it